<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rotterdam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rotterdam is the second-largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, on the Nieuwe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Maas</a> in the delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Meuse. It received city rights in 1340 and grew with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nieuwe-waterweg">Nieuwe Waterweg</a> of 1872 into the largest port in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, handling much of the trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. German bombing destroyed its centre on 14 May 1940, and it was rebuilt as a city of modern architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rotterdam.jpg" alt="Rotterdam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Oo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_view_of_Rotterdam,_taken_from_the_roof_of_the_Maassilo,_Rotterdam,_The_Netherlands.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rotterdam lies in the province of South Holland, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> delta, reached by the New Meuse channel, which was dug to connect first to the Meuse and now to the Rhine. The river Nieuwe Maas divides the city into a northern and a southern part, joined by tunnels and bridges. The Rhine, Meuse and Scheldt give waterway access into the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, including the industrialised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, and together with rail and road links they earned the city the nicknames Gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Gateway to the World.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement at the lower end of the fen stream Rotte, then called Rotta from words for muddy water, dates from at least the year 950. Around 1150 large floods ended development and led to the building of protective dikes and dams, including Schieland's High Sea Dike along the northern bank of the Nieuwe Maas. A dam on the Rotte was built in the 13th century, at the present-day Hoogstraat, and in 1340 William IV, Count of Holland, granted Rotterdam city rights.</p><p>Rotterdam has always been one of the main centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> shipping. Its Chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>, the world's first multinational, was established in 1602. Since 1795 it has hosted the chief congregation of the liberal Protestant Remonstrants, and its Catholic bishopric dates from 1955. The Dutch humanist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Desiderius Erasmus</a> is counted among its former inhabitants. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, because of Dutch neutrality and the city's position between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and German-occupied Belgium, it was the world's largest spy centre, and MI6 had its main European office on the Boompjes. On average 25,000 Belgian refugees lived there.</p><p>In the early stages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the German Luftwaffe bombed the city centre and destroyed many of its older buildings. After an initial crisis of reconstruction the centre became a site of ambitious new architecture. Earlier landmarks include the White House of 1898, the tallest office building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in its day, the Van Nelle Factory of 1929, a monument of modern factory design that became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2014, and Feyenoord's stadium De Kuip of 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rotterdam is the second-largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> by population and the largest by area, and it is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s largest seaport. It is the centre of the Rotterdam–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> metropolitan area, the most populous in the country. Erasmus University Rotterdam is named after Erasmus. The destruction of the city centre in 1940 gave it a varied architectural landscape, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">skyscrapers</a> designed by architects such as Rem Koolhaas, Piet Blom and Ben van Berkel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rotterdam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Industry expanded from the late nineteenth century: the Nieuwe Waterweg of 1872 and the North Sea Canal of 1876 opened <b>Rotterdam</b> and Amsterdam to large ships, and firms such as Royal Dutch Shell, Philips and Unilever became multinational giants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
